# Normalize EOL for all files that Git considers text files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Git LFS: binary game assets ---
*.png   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.jpg   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.webp  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.exr   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.blend filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.ogg   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.wav   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mp3   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.ttf   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.otf   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# --- The build journal's images are NOT LFS ---
# docs/chronicle/ is published to GitHub Pages, and actions/checkout does not fetch LFS by
# default — so an LFS-tracked PNG deploys as a 130-byte pointer file and the page serves
# text where an image should be. That is exactly what happened on the first deploy.
# Turning LFS on in the workflow instead would pull the ENTIRE sprite library (~31 MB of
# renders the page never uses) on every publish, against a 1 GB quota. These are a handful
# of screenshots; plain blobs are the right call, and it keeps the site self-contained.
docs/chronicle/assets/** -filter -diff -merge

# --- The fonts are NOT LFS, and this one is a correctness fix (LF-162) ---
# Four IBM Plex faces, 734 KB total. They are *source*, not build output — unlike the sprite
# renders, nothing regenerates them, so the churn argument for LFS does not apply and the
# whole set costs less than one atlas page.
#
# What made this worth doing rather than tolerating: `scripts/ui_theme.gd` (the `Ui` autoload)
# `preload()`s all four directly. Under any checkout that has not fetched LFS — a CI job with
# `lfs: false`, or a contributor who cloned before installing git-lfs — those `preload()`s
# resolve against 130-byte pointer TEXT, which is a genuine resource-load failure that
# cascades into every script reading a const off `Ui`: `hud.gd`, `menu.gd`, `pause_menu.gd`,
# `main.gd`, `draft.gd`. Five scripts fail for one missing 169 KB file, and the error names
# none of them.
#
# CI currently tolerates that by importing the project first, which makes the parse check
# pass while the FontFile still contains junk. Plain blobs make a clean checkout actually
# correct instead of merely parseable. Same reasoning as the chronicle images above.
assets/fonts/** -filter -diff -merge

# --- Godot / text: keep diffable ---
*.gd     text eol=lf
*.tscn   text eol=lf
*.tres   text eol=lf
*.import text eol=lf
*.svg    text eol=lf
